hi I'm Alexander Rossum the director of  the long now foundation I wanted to tell  you a little bit about the torpedo data  computer aboard the pampanito it was  first showed shown to me by Richard  Bikel knee he and Terry Lyndell restored  this computer to fully working order  it's one of the most complex mechanical  computers ever built we happen to have  this amazing example here in San  Francisco and not only is it the most  advanced and complex mechanical  computers or at least one of them it's  also the most compact is that they had  to be fit into a submarine so it had the  complexity of about 500 Swiss watches  and with life or death consequences  resulting from its results a very  incredible machine that did amazing  integrated equations on the fly to solve  a very difficult problem of the time  which is to correlate the position of  the submarine with the target and all  the mechanics of the torpedo all in real  time while both are moving what follows  as a piece by Terry Lyndell about how  this this machine works the TDC aboard  the pampanito is a mark 3 and one of  only two of its type installed in a  historic submarine the other is aboard  the USS both and in Honolulu pomp  Anita's TDC has been restored to fully  operational condition the TDC was unique  in world war ii is a computational part  of the first submerged integrated fire  control system that could track and  continuously aim torpedoes by setting  their gyro angles another thing that was  invented solely for this was basically  servos the idea that you could move us  move a move a motor on one end and then  have the motor on the other end do  exactly that same motion the TDC mark 3  gave the u.s. fleet submarine the  to fire torpedoes without first  estimating a future firing position  changing the ship's course or steering  into that position so instead of hoping  that nothing changed in the set up a  fleet submarine with the TDC could fire  at the target when the captain judged  the probability of making the hit to be  the greatest a world war 2 in World War  2  a torpedoes gyro angle was set  mechanically while it was in the - of a  shaft known that they call the spindle  slipped into the socket near the housing  of the torpedoes course gyroscope and  when the fire control system rotated the  shaft the gyroscope inside that torpedo  rotated so then after being fired the  torpedo travel in a straight course for  a known distance which they called the  reach with a delay and then the  torpedoes gyro was released and steered  the whole gyro after being activated by  a threaded shaft attached to the screws  once engaged the steering mechanism  brought the torpedo to a new course  based on the angular offset of the  gyroscope that was set by the TDC so the  mark 3 computer consisted of two  sections the position keeper and the  angle solver the position keeper tracked  the target and predicted its current  position to do this position creeper  automatically receive the input of the  ship's own course from the gyro compass  and the ship's speed from the pit log  the position keeper had cranks on its  face that set the targets length so how  long the ship was it's estimated speed  and the angle off the bow of the  submarine it also contained a sound  bearing converter that calculated the  targets location based on sonar  measurements so all the sensors of the  ship coming in to this one point and  being mechanically fed into a whole  series of gears  integrated into a single equation the  position keeper solved the equations of  motion integrated over time the result  was a continuous prediction of where the  target was at any instant successive  measurements of the targets position  were compared to the position keeper  predictions and corrections of error  were introduced with a hand-cranked so  this allowed people who are operating  the TDC to to enter in any corrections  that they felt were necessary because of  any issues that were going on outside of  these ships sensors the predicted target  position became more accurate as the  measurements made and corrections made  smaller so it was typical to get an  accurate track on the target after about  three or four observations under good  conditions the angle solver  automatically took the targets predicted  position from the position keeper  combined it with the tactical properties  of the torpedo and solved the torpedo  solve for the torpedoes gyro angle  values calculated from the solution were  returned to the position keeper in to  feedback loops so that then the gyro  automatically went to each of the  torpedo rooms and set the torpedo  continuously and so basically the TDC  is controlling both torpedo rooms  fore-and-aft and all torpedo tubes at  once so it is able to set through a  series of these servo motors the firing  solution for all ten torpedo tubes as  one a set of six I believe forward and  set of four aft that were all tracking  the target so at any point that captain  could launch a torpedo and any one of  those torpedoes would try and find the  target the US Navy thus had the system  that would point the torpedoes at a  target as the fire control problem  developed the TDC mark through was the  only torpedo targeting system of the  time that both solved for the gyro angle  and tracked the target in real time the  comparable system used by both Germany  and Japan could come  compute that and set the gyro angle for  a fixed time so they could only do it  for a fixed time that they tried to  predict in the future and then and then  fire on that time but they couldn't do  it continuously by tracking the target  so the idea of the piston the position  keeper and it's iterative reduction of  target position error was unique to the  US Navy and represented a really  distinct advantage one of the other cool  things that's inside of there are these  three-dimensional cams that allow it to  do kind of integrated math and this is  similar to a three-dimensional cam that  we use in the 10,000 Year clock to solve  for the equation of time as it evolves  over 10,000 years so you might want to  keep a lookout for both of those thank  you
 

